Measured: a TCP peer that accepts the connection and then never answers makes any karr command hang indefinitely. Test ran 300s and was only ended by an external timeout wrapper; the process sits in state S at ~1.8% CPU, 47MB RSS — it never returns on its own.

Root cause: fetch/push try the native libgit2 path FIRST (Git.pm:1152 fetch, Git.pm:1269 push). KARR_TRANSPORT_TIMEOUT (default 120s, Git.pm:2023) only ever applies to _cli_transport, i.e. AFTER the native call has returned. If it never returns, the timeout is unreachable.

libgit2 1.9 does support GIT_OPT_SET_SERVER_TIMEOUT (48) and GIT_OPT_SET_SERVER_CONNECT_TIMEOUT (47), both default 0 = no limit. They are NOT usable from here: Git::Libgit2::FFI.pm:101 declares git_libgit2_opts only as (int) => (int, string), while these options need (int, int). So this ticket spans two distributions:
  1. ~/dev/p5-git-libgit2: add an (int,int) variadic attachment + the two constants.
  2. karr: set both timeouts once at repo open, derived from KARR_TRANSPORT_TIMEOUT so one knob governs both transports.

Impact: this is the "hang" that blocks unattended agents. Worst case per command today: sync_before 3 attempts + sync_after 3 + SyncGuard 3 = up to 9 x 120s = 18 minutes via CLI fallback, unbounded via native.
2026-08-15 09:10 Fixed across two repos, both committed locally, nothing pushed.

p5-git-libgit2 8611bfc "Bind git_libgit2_opts for its (int, int) shape, for the network timeouts": second attachment of the same variadic C function under the Perl name git_libgit2_opts_int, plus the constants GIT_OPT_SET_SERVER_CONNECT_TIMEOUT and GIT_OPT_SET_SERVER_TIMEOUT in @EXPORT_OK. Their values are 39 and 41, not the 47/48 this ticket named -- measured against libgit2 1.9.0 (positions in /usr/include/git2/common.h; 47 and 48 return -1, "invalid option"). New t/28-server-timeout.t proves the option works rather than only returning 0: git_remote_connect to a socket that listens and never accepts has to end on its own, run in a forked child with the parent enforcing the deadline. t/26-constants.t pins the two enum positions. GET_* counterparts stay unbound: a pointer in a variadic argument list segfaults FFI::Platypus 2.11.

karr 5b2d24c "Bound the native transport too, not just the CLI fallback (#170)": App::karr::Git::_apply_native_transport_timeouts sets both options from KARR_TRANSPORT_TIMEOUT when _repo opens the repository (seconds -> ms, 0 still means no limit, sub-ms budgets floored at 1 ms so they cannot round into "no limit"). Package-level latch keyed on the value, because git_libgit2_opts mutates process-global libgit2 state. cpanfile now requires Git::Libgit2 0.007. New t/170-native-transport-timeout.t: silent listener + git:// origin + KARR_NO_CLI_FALLBACK=1, fetch in a forked child; it fails after ~3 s with KARR_TRANSPORT_TIMEOUT=3, and the second subtest is the counter-proof that with 0 it still hangs. Red step verified: with the one call removed the test fails (child killed after 25 s).

MEASURED GAP -- ssh:// is NOT covered. libgit2 applies these timeouts to its own socket transports only: git:// and http:// time out as configured, ssh:// was still blocked after 75 s with both options set (libssh2 does its own reads). So this bounds the native path for git/http/https; for ssh the git CLI fallback stays the only bounded route. Filed as #174 with the repro and three options for fixing it. Second limit: needs libgit2 1.8+; bookworm 1.5.1 (karr CI) rejects the options -- deliberately no warning, since it would fire on every purely local command; the requirement is stated in the Git.pm POD.

